WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, Episode 13.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.480
It's June 24, 2026, and I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.000
Here's what's happening at SpaceX today.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.680
Vandenberg site clearance and a Texas court ruling together removed two longstanding constraints

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
on West Coast and Texas launch rates.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.340
Crews have started tearing down the old SLC-6 complex and nearby Delta and Titan buildings

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.280
at Vandenberg Space Force Base.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.400
The cleared land directly opens room for new pads and processing buildings that can

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.520
support higher Falcon Heavy and Starship class flight rates from the West Coast.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.060
Observers tracking the work through public satellite imagery note how quickly heavy equipment

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:41.840
has moved in.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.920
While the West Coast gains physical space, Texas just removed a legal one.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.940
The Texas Supreme Court upheld SpaceX authority to close Boca Chica Beach during Falcon and

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.120
Starship operations.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.400
Ending years of litigation from local residents.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.160
That decision eliminates a frequent source of last-minute launch window changes and lets

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.020
Starbase plan tighter intervals between flights.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.460
Local accounts have already posted drone footage showing beach access points reopening

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.860
right after the ruling.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:19.500
Rakuten's CEO stated that AST Space Mobile's Direct to Cell satellite network is essential

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.860
for Japanese national security communications resilience.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.960
The comment highlights competitive pressure on SpaceX's own Direct to Cell Starlink service

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.580
in the Asia Pacific region.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.880
The statement came as ASTS stock jumped overnight on the remarks.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.120
Reports linked the push directly to resilience needs in Japan amid ongoing rivalry with

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.200
Starlink offerings.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.300
The development places additional focus on regional spectrum and partnership timelines

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.360
for both companies.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:56.800
Observers note the quote, positions AST explicitly against SpaceX expansion plans in the same

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:57.200
market.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.640
With both coasts now freer to increase cadence, the next bottleneck is orbital refueling

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.280
at scale.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:11.100
On the AI front, SpaceX has confirmed the name Starmind for a planned constellation of orbital

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.460
AI satellites that could reach 1 million compute nodes.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.600
The system would move inference workloads into space by taking advantage of Starship payload

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.740
capacity for large scale deployment.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.320
Early plans show the constellation sharing orbital planes and ground stations with upgraded

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.300
Starlink hardware to gain latency and power benefits.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.900
The same vacuum environment that enables Starmind also changes the thermodynamics of compute.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.780
From an engineering standpoint, placing inference hardware in orbit removes the need for

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.000
convective and conductive cooling that dominates ground data centers.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.160
Radiation becomes the only heat rejection path, so nodes can run at higher sustained power

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.840
density without multi-megawatt-chiller plants.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:03.580
The tradeoff is added launch mass plus radiation hardening, yet the marginal cost per inference

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.900
token drops once nodes are on station because incremental Starship flights replace ongoing

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.160
terrestrial power and water expansion.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.400
That same refueling and reuse tempo is also the hinge for NASA's lunar plans.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.340
Worth keeping an eye on, NASA's current Artemis architecture still calls for 15 Starship

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.600
flights per crew landing to deliver propellant and cargo.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.380
The plan depends on rapid reuse and orbital refueling that have not been shown at that

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.120
pace yet.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.160
Any shortfall in either capability would push back crewed landings and could push NASA toward

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.620
extra commercial landers.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:46.240
NASA's lunar landing plan still hinges on 15 Starship launches per mission, yet orbital

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.300
refueling and rapid booster reuse remain unproven at that scale.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.660
Any slip in either capability directly delays Artemis timelines and forces NASA to consider

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.020
alternative architectures or additional commercial landers.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.920
The gap between required flight rate and demonstrated performance is the clearest near-term risk to

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:06.780
the current manifest.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.700
Spaceflight analysts are debating whether the 15 launch lunar architecture can be compressed

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.700
if Starship refueling demonstrations succeed on the next two flights.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:23.300
The architecture assumes rapid reuse and orbital refueling that have not yet been demonstrated

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.500
at that tempo.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.800
While lunar timelines are still being stress tested, routine Falcon flights continue without

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:31.120
pause.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.720
This week's manifest includes a Falcon 9 alongside the first Pegasus XL launch in years,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.440
and several Chinese long-Mart missions.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:47.320
Several Falcon 9 boosters have now flown more than 30 times, extending the reuse sequence

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.580
that has become routine.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.780
A Falcon 9 ascent in the coming days will pass over the Phoenix area, giving observers

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.460
a visible plume and possible sonic booms depending on exact time and path.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.040
Launch enthusiasts are already comparing the expected viewing geometry to earlier nighttime

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.480
ascents from Vandenberg.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.340
Amateur radio operators are also gearing up to capture telemetry from the Pegasus XL and

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.980
contrast its small payload profile with Falcon 9 performance.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:23.260
The week's global manifest lists six orbital attempts, with one Falcon 9 flight scheduled alongside

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:28.800
the first Pegasus XL mission in years, and multiple Chinese long-march launches.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.160
The Falcon 9 will continue the booster reuse sequence that has now exceeded 30 flights

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.280
on several vehicles.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:42.160
Amateur radio operators are preparing to log telemetry from the Pegasus XL mission, and contrasting

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.000
its small payload profile with Falcon 9's routine mass to orbit.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.040
Observers on X are tracking demolition progress at Vandenberg through public domain satellite

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.080
imagery, and noting the speed of heavy equipment mobilization.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:02.120
Launch enthusiasts are discussing the Phoenix area viewing geometry for the upcoming Falcon 9,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.700
and comparing it to prior nighttime ascents from Vandenberg.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.900
On the business side, investors are watching the same cadence metrics.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:15.540
Key markers ahead include the first crewed Starship flight, Starlink subscriber targets,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.660
and Rising Raptor production rates.

